BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Vibe Coding: Make Your Own Interactive Story with AI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6a9b4e24aafa61002961b09b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What if you could write a story where the reader chooses what 
 happens\, like a branching adventure\, a mystery\, or a game\, and have AI
  build it with you?\nIn this class\, you'll learn "vibe-coding": using an 
 AI coding assistant to build your own interactive story or simple game. Yo
 u'll design the branches\, describe the rules in plain language\, test for
  the tricky edge cases (what if the player picks the weird option?)\, and 
 end up with a single file you can share with anyone. Just a story you want
  to tell. This is the playful sibling of the practical vibe-coding worksho
 p.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. Computers ar
 e provided. No coding experience required. If you wish to save your work
  from this hands-on program\, please bring your own USB stick.\nRegistrati
 on is required and will start September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your s
 pot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nA
 fter this time\, remaining seats will be filled on a first come\, first se
 rve basis until capacity is reached.\nAbout the AI Tools Used in This Prog
 ram\nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI tools (listed be
 low) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your own account for t
 his program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Resident’s guida
 nce throughout sessions\, which include best practices on using AI.\nWe en
 courage you to review the privacy statements of each tool prior to attendi
 ng this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy p
 olicy\nGoogle Antigravity\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy polic
 y\nGoogle AI Studio\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nInf
 ormation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is coll
 ected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, secti
 ons 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event
  and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
 \nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2
 G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e24aafa61002961b09b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Série Portraits et autoportraits: Du Symbolisme à l’Expression
 nisme : le moi en question.
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T153000
UID:6aa17e8b8cf597003f9241c1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:En un temps où l’industrialisation est triomphante\, certai
 ns peintres et écrivains sont\, en cette fin du XIXe siècle\, en proie 
 à une inattendue inquiétude existentielle : le rationalisme explique-t-i
 l à lui-seul le sens de la vie et de l’histoire ? Ces interrogations se
 ront parfaitement résumées par le titre du tableau que Paul Gauguin exé
 cute en 1898 :\nD’où venons-nous ? Que sommes-nous ? Où allons-nous ?\
 nDe Van Gogh à George Grosz en passant par Edvard Munch\, les peintres ex
 plorent alors dans leurs autoportraits ces champs d’inquiétude qui étr
 eignent les consciences\, en de fascinants tableaux hallucinés.\nCette co
 nférence vous est proposée par Gilles Genty\, historien de l’art\, en 
 partenariat avec l’\nAlliance française de Toronto\n.\nAccessibilité:\
 nLe sous-titrage en direct ou automatique\, l’interprétation en langue 
 des signes\, les services d’intervenant\, ou les services d’accompagne
 ment sont disponibles sur demande. Veuillez soumettre votre demande au mo
 ins deux semaines à l’avance à : tpl.ca/accessibility-events\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa17e8b8cf597003f9241c1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
