BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Smaller and More Robotic
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T235959
UID:6a91bb035e6fc10d0550658d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of September\, the works of artist Jay R
 angam are on display at Oakwood Village Library Arts Centre.\nJay Rangam 
 is an artist that lives and works in Toronto\, Canada. He received his MFA
  from the Maryland Institute College of Art in 2021 and his BFA\nfrom OCAD
  University in 2019. He has exhibited in group shows in Toronto\, Montreal
 \, Baltimore and New York.\nMy recent paintings from 2024 - 2025 begin wit
 h digitally combining drawings that I’ve used in previous bodies of work
  to create new images. Using\nthe digital cartoon as a starting point for 
 painting\, the application of different materials and colours is intended 
 to abstract the representational subject\nmatter of the drawings\, creatin
 g paintings that appear somewhat abstract while retaining traces of a rela
 tionship to the natural world.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit
  Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists t
 o exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to r
 eflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\
 nThe exhibit can be viewed during Deer Park's open hours. Please check the
  Oakwood Village branch page before your visit. The branch will be closed
  on September 6th\, 7th\, and 30th.\nFree. All are welcome.\nTitle of Piec
 e: Garden\nMedium (e.g. oil\, acrylic\,\nphotography\, etc.): gouache on c
 anvas\nSize/Dimensions: Extra Large
LOCATION:Oakwood Village Library and Arts Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a91bb035e6fc10d0550658d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI\, Our Community and What Happens Next
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T160000
UID:6a9b51d02dac6e0037243876@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Twelve weeks of making\, testing\, and learning. Now what do w
 e do with it?\nJoin Innovator in Residence Ryan Kelln for his closing even
 t\, as he reflects on the Applied AI residency: the projects people built\
 , the unexpected moments that taught us the most\, and the needs we discov
 ered but couldn't solve. We'll tell our stories. Then it's your turn. In s
 mall groups\, we'll ask: what should we continue\, what should we change? 
 Help turn what we learned into commitments to the future: what this commun
 ity could build and nurture together\, in fruitful and responsible collabo
 ration with AI.\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\, 2026
  at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of t
 he start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining seats will be filled
  on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity is reached.\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the lib
 rary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nansw
 erline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51d02dac6e0037243876
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI At Home: No Cloud\, No Account
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T200000
UID:6a9b51cb1c197d11325d708b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What if your AI didn't need the internet at all: no subscripti
 on\, no one recording what you type?\nIn this class\, you'll see AI models
  running entirely on one computer\, with no connection and no data leaving
  the room. We'll compare a small local model against a powerful cloud mode
 l and plain search: what each gets right\, what each costs\, and what the 
 privacy trade-offs really are. You'll leave with a decision sheet for your
  own hardware\, an installation recipe\, and an honest look at what "open"
  really means when it comes to AI.\nRegistration is required and will star
 t November 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed a
 fter 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining s
 eats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity is 
 reached.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this
  form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librarie
 s Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be u
 sed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Question
 s about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answer
 line staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Tor
 onto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51cb1c197d11325d708b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bring Your Problem: Can AI Help?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T200000
UID:6a9b519baafa61002961b295@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Do you have a recurring problem you think AI might help with? 
 A thing you keep doing by hand that feels like it should be easier? Bring 
 it. If you don't have one\, come help your neighbours with theirs.\nIn thi
 s interactive class\, you'll learn a simple seven-step way to think about 
 problems: what's annoying you\, what you've tried\, what AI could actually
  do\, and where it would fail. We'll gather real problems from the room\, 
 sort them into groups\, and map out what's worth solving with AI\, and wha
 t's better solved without it. We'll also talk honestly about what an AI fi
 x costs: your money\, your time\, and sometimes your private information. 
 The problems AI could help with most are often the most personal\, and the
  most personal ones are worth the most care.\nRegistration is required and
  will start October 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be gua
 ranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, re
 maining seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until cap
 acity is reached.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informatio
 n on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public
  Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will 
 only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation.
  Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed 
 to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Str
 eet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b519baafa61002961b295
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Peek Inside an AI Assistant: How It Thinks and Works
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T180000
UID:6a9b51c6aafa61002961b2ab@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:You've asked an AI chatbot questions. But there's another kind
  of AI that doesn't just answer: it acts.\nIn this demo-driven talk\, we'l
 l look deeply at always-on personal AI assistants. The assistant can read 
 your files\, use your apps\, and remember what you've told it\, growing mo
 re useful over time. The same powers that let it help you can hurt you if 
 something goes wrong. We'll also open up what happens inside: the hidden i
 nstructions\, the tools it uses\, what it remembers and forgets.\nThen we'
 ll look at the AI assistant that helped develop this workshop\, and how we
  shaped our workflow together\, and why.\nRegistration is required and wil
 l start November 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaran
 teed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remai
 ning seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until capaci
 ty is reached.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information o
 n this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Li
 braries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will onl
 y be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Qu
 estions about how the library handles your information can be directed to 
 Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street
 \, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51c6aafa61002961b2ab
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Do AIs "Think"? And Why They Get Things Wrong
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T160000
UID:6a9b51cdaafa61002961b2ad@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:How does a machine actually "think"? Explore the history and l
 atest developments of machine learning.\nIn this class\, we'll dig into wh
 at's really going on inside AI systems: how they decide what matters in yo
 ur question\, where their training data comes from (and who's missing from
  it)\, and how hidden instructions can sneak into content and hijack an AI
 . Develop an intuitive understanding of how AIs "think" without the math. 
 We'll talk about how AIs remember\, how they don't and how they are both s
 imilar and different to humans. And we'll ask the question underneath it a
 ll: is there anyone home inside the machine?\nRegistration is required and
  will start November 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be gu
 aranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, r
 emaining seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until ca
 pacity is reached.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informati
 on on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Publi
 c Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will
  only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation
 . Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed
  to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge St
 reet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51cdaafa61002961b2ad
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Public Speaking for Newcomers: Communicate with Confidence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T140000
UID:6a92f312aafa610029600470@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Communicating clearly and confidently is a skill that everyone
  should have. For newcomers\, public speaking can feel especially daunting
  when navigating a new language or cultural expectations. This session is 
 designed to help you overcome those challenges and approach public speakin
 g with confidence. Along many educational and professional paths in Canada
 \, giving presentations is often a requirement. This session will provide 
 an overview of how to design and deliver presentations\, while also offeri
 ng practical tips and techniques for speaking clearly and effectively in a
  new environment.\nThis program is part of Small Business Month and the Ne
 wcomer Entrepreneurship Initiative.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe P
 ersonal Information on this form is collection by the Toronto Public Libra
 ry under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). Th
 e information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliv
 ery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informati
 on can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5
 981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.​
LOCATION:Woodside Square\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a92f312aafa610029600470
